25. Under the referral arrangement, a UCB shall provide physical infrastructure within its branch premises to insurance companies for selling their insurance products to the UCB’s customers with adequate disclosure and transparency, and in turn earn referral fees on the basis of premia collected. The above permission shall be subject to the following conditions:
A UCB shall comply with the relevant IRDAI regulations for undertaking referral business with insurance companies;
A UCB shall not adopt any restrictive practice of compelling its customers to opt for a particular insurance company, particularly in respect of assets financed by the UCB. The customers shall be allowed to exercise their own choice of insurer;
A UCB desirous of entering into referral arrangement, in addition to complying with IRDAI regulations, shall also enter into an agreement with the concerned insurance company for allowing use of its premises and making use of the existing infrastructure of the UCB;
The initial agreement shall be for a maximum period of three years at the first instance and the UCB shall have the discretion to renegotiate the terms or replace it by another agreement after the initial period, depending on its satisfaction with the service;
Upon completion of the initial period, a UCB shall be free to sign a longer-term contract with the approval of its Board;

28. A UCB shall be eligible to apply for a license as Authorized Dealers (AD) Category I / II subject to compliance with the following norms:
(1) AD Category-I
A UCB shall be included in the Second Schedule to RBI Act 1934;
A UCB shall have a minimum net worth of ₹200 crore;
The CRAR shall not be less than 12 percent;
The gross NPA shall not exceed 10 percent;
A UCB shall have made profit in the preceding three years;
A UCB shall have no defaults in maintenance of CRR / SLR requirements;
A UCB shall have sound internal control systems; and
A UCB shall have an elected board of management with at least two professionals.
(2) AD Category II
A UCB shall have a minimum net worth of ₹10 crore;
The CRAR shall not be less than nine percent;
The gross NPAs shall not exceed 10 percent;
A UCB shall have made profit in the preceding three years;
A UCB shall have no defaults in maintenance of CRR / SLR requirements;
A UCB shall have sound internal control systems; and
A UCB shall have an elected board of management with at least two professional directors.

29. A UCB fulfilling the above-mentioned eligibility criterion and willing to apply for AD authorisation shall submit its application through Pravaah Portal (https://pravaah.rbi.org.in) to Department of Regulation, RBI with the document / information as specified under:
A copy of the latest audited balance sheet;
Net worth computation and a certificate from a Chartered Accountant certifying the same;
Certified copies of the balance sheets and profit and loss accounts of the UCB for the last three years;
A declaration to the effect that no proceedings have been initiated by any law enforcing agency against the UCB or its Directors;
A Board resolution for conducting AD Category-I/II activities and for applying to RBI for the license;
A report on KYC / AML systems prevalent in the UCB; and
Details of the availability of skilled manpower and infrastructure, the internal control / audit system and risk management systems put in place by the UCB.

